\f1\fs22 The Johnny strode through the laboratory wing of Sublime Point, his confidence having grown in proportion to the distance he maintained from Sarah. The three Sages\'97Kleo, Solo, and Chilo\'97trailed behind him, their robes incongruous among the sterile lab equipment and holographic displays.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip Stockwell lingered near the back of the group, trying to appear inconspicuous despite his new Shakti Enterprises lab coat. The former Stasis Anomalies Investigator kept nervously adjusting his security badge, acutely wondering if his former employers knew where he was.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91As you can see, gentlemen,\'92 The Johnny said with the practiced charm of someone who'd spent a lifetime taking credit for others\'92 work, \'91we\'92ve made significant progress in decoding the Gyges Disc's properties. If you'll observe these readings..."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He gestured expansively toward a hovering display where patterns of concentric circles pulsed and shifted, their edges blurring and reforming in hypnotic rhythm. Dr. Quark stood nearby, his posture stiff with the awkwardness of a scientist suddenly required to perform for visitors.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip's eyes widened at the display. The patterns matched classified Fridge Force files he'd only glimpsed during an incident briefing\'97one where three senior agents had suffered unexplained seizures just from viewing similar recordings.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Fascinating,\'92 Chilo murmured, stepping closer to the display than protocol would normally allow. Unlike his fellow Sages, whose attention remained politely distant, Chilo's eyes locked onto the patterns with intensity. \'91And you believe these are messages?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Not believe\'97know,\'92 The Johnny corrected, enjoying the rare opportunity to correct someone else. "Dr. Quark has confirmed that each pattern corresponds to a universe with a different universal gravitational constant.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo nodded slowly, as if absorbing this information for the first time.\'92Have you attempted to adjust your instruments to compensate for temporal variance as well as gravitational?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dr. Quark frowned. \'91Temporal variance?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That's extremely dangerous,\'92 Chip blurted out before he could stop himself. All eyes turned to him, and he immediately regretted speaking. \'91I mean... Fridge Force has... that is... stasis technology operates on similar principles, and temporal calibration has been known to cause... incidents.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Nonsense,\'92 The Johnny said then, after a moment of reflection while everyone stared at him, raised an eyebrow. \'91What kind of incidents?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip swallowed hard. \'91Classified.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91A mere suggestion,\'92 Shilo said smoothly, his voice dropping to a casual murmur. \'91If gravity differs between universes, surely time flows differently as well. Perhaps a matrix calibration accounting for relative chronological displacement might\'85 but I'm no physicist.\'92 He waved his hand dismissively at his own suggestion.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And what do Fridge Force regulations say about such calibrations, Mr. Stockwell?\'92 Chilo asked innocently.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip felt trapped between his new obligation to Shakti Enterprises and the dangers of crossing Fridge Force. \'91The 
\f2\i Refrigeration Secrets Act
\f1\i0  prohibits me from discussing specific technical details, but...\'92 he lowered his voice, \'91there's a reason stasis fields are locally contained and never allowed to resonate with one another.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Johnny's eyes narrowed. He glanced at Dr. Quark, whose expression had shifted from confusion to consideration.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"It's an interesting thought," Dr. Quark admitted. "We could modify the phase variance compensator to include a temporal coefficient, with appropriate safety measures."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Precisely what I was about to suggest," The Johnny said quickly. "Let's implement that immediately."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"I strongly advise against this," Chip said, his face pale. "At minimum, we should conduct a full containment assessment before\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Mr. Stockwell," The Johnny interrupted, "your expertise in refrigeration is noted, but this is cutting-edge physics, not food preservation. Dr. Quark, proceed with the modifications."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As the scientists turned their attention to the equipment, Solo drew close to Chilo. "What are you doing?" he whispered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Accelerating inevitability," Chilo replied, his voice barely audible.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"This isn't what we discussed," Solo hissed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo merely smiled, watching as The Johnny began directing lab technicians to modify their instruments according to calculations he clearly didn't understand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip stood frozen in place, internal alarms blaring. He'd seen the classified reports on "temporal resonance events". But revealing that would mean admitting to knowledge he wasn't supposed to have. Instead, he quietly slipped away, heading for his office to compose an urgent message to Sarah Jacobson.\

